## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

The cause comes from the tenant and the way the report came in (`causes.ts`, `problems.ts`), not
from intake's tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and its tags disagree with the cause
in both big groups. The export repeats some reports across pages; every count here is one id, one report.
Counts follow the desk's view: a report stays under the cause it is filed under. The one-day moves
are filed under `parser` and also counted under `shift`, so the rows add up to more than the ticket.

## parser → [#1](https://github.com/ArielZgorski/megadev/issues/1)

The shared parser reads every date month first. All <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" of="tenants" /> tenants on file write the day first,
so day and month swap: 7 April goes out as 4 July. This is the fix #4471 has queued. It covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" /> reports, by app, email and the booking-v2 form, counted as filed:
that includes the one-day moves below.

<ReportList ticket={4471} problem="parser" limit={3} />

## hint → [#2](https://github.com/ArielZgorski/megadev/issues/2)

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Its tenants write the day first.
People type what the hint tells them, the form reads it the tenant's way, and day and month swap.
The parser fix does not touch this: the form reads the date day first, as it should, after the
hint has made people type it wrong. Fix the hint per tenant locale. <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports, all through the form, from <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" of="tenants" /> tenants on booking-v3.

<ReportList ticket={4471} problem="hint" limit={3} />

## shift → [#3](https://github.com/ArielZgorski/megadev/issues/3)

These dates are not swapped. They move by one day, and the day depends on where the reader is:
one meeting showed Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto. A day/month parser cannot do that; a
time zone or daylight-saving conversion can. The desk read them as a one-day move
(`desk-notes.json`) but filed them under `parser` by tenant, so the parser fix would close them
unfixed. They stay counted there and count here too. <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="shift" /> reports:

<ReportList ticket={4471} problem="shift" />

## Engineering update

#4471 closed on 5 October.

- `parser` · fixed · `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` → #1 closed, done.
- `hint` · won't fix · booking-v3 form retired → #2 closed, not planned.
- `shift` · not in the update → #3 stays open. A day-first parse does not touch a one-day move.

## What changes for the desk

- Closing #4471 does not close the one-day move. #3 is the open part of it.
- Merging on subject (decision 0001) put different bugs in one thread: the subjects are the same words
  for different faults. Before merging a big ticket, group its reports by cause, and read the
  ones that do not fit.

